\title{Short-Window Forecasting of Pyroclastic Density Currents at
Mount Rinar: Tremor Precursors and Hazard Communication}
\author{S.~Okafor, M.~Tedesco, and L.~Haran}
\date{}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Pyroclastic density currents are the deadliest products of explosive
eruptions, yet their onset remains difficult to anticipate more than a
few days in advance. At Mount Rinar we show that a sustained rise in
volcanic tremor amplitude preceded the three most recent current-forming
episodes by two to four days, and we outline how this signal is now used
in the provincial civil protection bulletin.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Mount Rinar is an andesitic stratovolcano whose northern and eastern
flanks are incised by steep drainages that funnel pyroclastic density
currents toward settled land. Forecasting efforts have historically
focused on deformation and gas emissions, with mixed success.

\section{Monitoring Network and Precursory Signals}
The observatory operates twelve broadband seismic stations on the
edifice. Since 2019, each current-forming episode has been preceded by a
slow, persistent increase in tremor amplitude lasting several days,
without a corresponding increase in deformation.

\section{Plain-Language Summary for the Civil Protection Bulletin}
\input{bulletin}

\section{Conclusions}
Tremor-based short-window forecasts are now issued weekly to provincial
authorities. The approach does not predict eruption size, and false
alarms remain possible, but the lead time is sufficient to stage
evacuation transport for the most exposed settlements.

\end{document}
